% bool_clause, satisfiable, and the search must guess WRONG before it succeeds.
%
% Two things this model is built to do, neither of which the Boolean suite had before
% (M2-T1/M2-T2 shipped its propagators with nothing that ran them):
%
% 1. THE ANSWER DEPENDS ON THE CONSTRAINT. The default search of SPEC 3.4 is
%    input_order + indomain_min, and a `var bool' is the order encoding on [0, 1]
%    (D-0007), so indomain_min tries FALSE first for every Boolean. A model whose
%    answer is all-false is therefore solved just as well by a solver that ignores
%    every Boolean constraint it is given. Here `a' is true, which is the value the
%    search reaches last, so the answer is evidence that the clauses were posted and
%    ran -- an ignored `bool_clause' would print `a = false'.
%
% 2. THE PROOF CONTAINS A DERIVATION. A satisfiable run only logs a nogood if a branch
%    fails first (D-0017, and test/models/guess_wrong_sat.fzn is the integer mirror of
%    this point), so a SAT model that root-propagates straight to its answer emits a
%    proof with no `rup' in it at all and tests the .opb rows only. Here neither clause
%    is unit at the root, the search decides a = false, `a \/ b' makes b true,
%    `a \/ ~b' is then violated, and that conflict is what forces a = true. So the
%    emitted proof carries bool_clause's unit-propagation reason AND its conflict
%    reason, both of which veripb has to accept.
%
% Ground truth, independent of the search: a \/ b and a \/ ~b together entail a, and
% with a true both clauses are satisfied whatever b is, so indomain_min settles b at
% false. Unique under the default strategy; a = true is forced under any strategy.
var bool: a :: output_var;
var bool: b :: output_var;
constraint bool_clause([a, b], []);
constraint bool_clause([a], [b]);
solve satisfy;
